<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">United Democratic Party (Belize)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1973">1973</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The United Democratic Party is one of the two main political parties of Belize, formed in 1973 by the merger of opposition groups. It has alternated in power with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-united-party">People's United Party</a>, governing under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-esquivel">Manuel Esquivel</a> in 1984–1989 and 1993–1998 and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dean-barrow">Dean Barrow</a> from 2008 to 2020.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-democratic-party-belize.jpg" alt="United Democratic Party (Belize)" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Fuebar at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Belize_United_Democratic_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1973 the opposition in Belize was weak, and the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-united-party">People's United Party</a> had never lost a legislative election since its foundation. The main opposition parties, the National Independence Party and the People's Development Movement, met with a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> to consider an alliance, and their merger formed the United Democratic Party on 27 September 1973. Controversially, a large part of the United Black Association for Development also voted to join. The party was founded as a centre-right conservative party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In its first general election, in 1974, the UDP stood candidates everywhere except Corozal District, where it backed the Corozal United Front, and won six seats, coming within 18 votes of three more. Dean Lindo then became leader. The party did well in municipal elections in the 1970s but lost the 1979 general election, falling to five seats; Lindo lost his own seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-musa">Said Musa</a> and was replaced by Theodore Aranda. Aranda was removed in late 1982 and succeeded by Curl Thompson, and later by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-esquivel">Manuel Esquivel</a>, former leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a>.</p><p>The UDP won the Belize City Council in December 1983 and the general election of 1984, taking 21 of 28 seats, and Esquivel became the party's first prime minister. It lost in 1989 by 13 seats to 15, but for the 1993 election it formed an alliance with the National Alliance for Belizean Rights, which won 16 of 29 seats. After a heavy defeat in 1998, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-united-party">People's United Party</a> took 26 of 29 seats, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dean-barrow">Dean Barrow</a> replaced Esquivel as leader.</p><p>The party won only seven seats in 2003 but returned to power in 2008 with 25 of 31 seats after ten years in opposition. Barrow called the election of November 2020 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a>, which with the effects of Hurricane Eta had left many voters discontented, and the UDP won only 5 of 31 seats, one of the worst results in its history. It kept the same number of seats in the general election of 2025.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United Democratic Party (Belize)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Power has alternated between the People's United Party and the <b>United Democratic Party</b>, under prime ministers such as Manuel Esquivel, Said Musa, Dean Barrow and Johnny Briceño.</blockquote><a href="/state/belize">Belize</a></li></ul></section>
